.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-init 1  "tapectl-init " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-init \- Make a new archive: catalog, config, operator tenant, keys, escrow
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl init\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-operator\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-escrow\fR] [\fB\-\-escrow\-public\-key\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-operator\-key\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Make a new archive: catalog, config, operator tenant, keys, escrow
.PP
Creates the database, config.toml, the operator tenant with its keys, and the permanent escrow recipient (ADR\-0005), whose secret it prints once. \-\-no\-escrow skips the escrow recipient; \-\-escrow\-public\-key adopts an existing one instead of minting a new identity. It ends with the next steps.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-operator\fR \fI<OPERATOR>\fR
Name of the operator tenant (default: the login name)

Defaults to the login name ($USER), except under a system account (a uid below UID_MIN in /etc/login.defs, such as a `tapectl` service user), where it is required: `init` refuses rather than name the operator after the account.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-escrow\fR
Do not create the escrow recipient now

By default `init` generates it (ADR\-0005) and prints its secret once. Use this only when you will adopt an existing escrow identity with `key import \-\-escrow` instead, or in tests and tooling that register escrow separately.
.TP
\fB\-\-escrow\-public\-key\fR \fI<KEY_OR_FILE>\fR
Adopt this escrow public key instead of minting one

An age1… literal, or a path to a .pub file. The disaster\-recovery form: a rebuilt machine adopts the original recipient from the heir kit\*(Aqs cover sheet so every tape\*(Aqs receipts keep matching.
.TP
\fB\-\-operator\-key\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Adopt this age identity file as the operator\*(Aqs key

The SECRET, used instead of minting a pair, as `tenant add \-\-adopt\-key` does for a tenant: stored byte for byte as keys/<operator>\-primary.age.key, mode 0600, and nothing is minted. A path only, never a key on the command line. Refused before anything is created when it is not one X25519 identity, or when it is the secret of \-\-escrow\-public\-key.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
